    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "## Problem\n\nSince #37907 the markdown ftplugin starts treesitter unconditionally, so classic syntax never loads for markdown buffers and `g:markdown_fenced_languages` has no effect. Fenced code blocks are only highlighted for languages with a bundled parser (c, lua, markdown, query, vim, vimdoc); everything else — python, py, html, bash=sh, ruby, perl, javascript, coffee, csv — is silently unhighlighted.\n\n`syntax.txt` (`ft-markdown-syntax`) still documents the variable, and its own example `g:markdown_fenced_languages = ['html', 'python', 'bash=sh']` is entirely non-functional: none of those three has a bundled parser.\n\n## Repro (nvim 0.12.4, default config, no plugins)\n\n````markdown\n```c\nint main() { return 0; }\n```\n\n```python\ndef hello():\n    print(\"hi\")\n```\n````\n\nThe `c` fence is highlighted (`return`, `int`), the `python` fence is not (`def` is plain). Verified headless: `c.so` is bundled and `queries/c/highlights.scm` yields 42 captures; there is no `python.so`/`csv.so` anywhere and no `queries/python/` dir, so the injection parses to nothing.\n\n(Display note: GitHub's own renderer happens to show the same split in the repro block above — the ```` ```c ```` part is highlighted, the ```` ```python ```` part is not. That is a quirk of the markdown highlighter, not the bug; the point is the same content is plain in nvim.)\n\n## Why it is silent\n\n`vim.treesitter.get_parser(buf, 'python')` returns a lazy LanguageTree even when no parser file exists; parsing fails at parse time with no error. Users just see dark code blocks with no way to know why — which is likely why this was never reported.\n\n## Affected\n\n- plugin-less users: nvim 0.12 bundles parsers specifically to make treesitter-first work out of the box, so the default experience is broken for any language outside the bundled set;\n- vim migrants carrying `g:markdown_fenced_languages` in their vimrc;\n- csv specifically can never get per-column highlighting via treesitter (the official grammar is column-agnostic by design; per-column exists only in a zed fork), so csv.vim's classic rendering is the only path — and it is unreachable.\n\n## Proposal\n\nPR #41336: skip `vim.treesitter.start()` in `runtime/ftplugin/markdown.lua` when `g:markdown_fenced_languages` is set, restoring classic syntax for fenced blocks.\n\nAlternative worth considering regardless: warn when an injected fence language has no parser installed.\n\n## Environment\n\n- nvim 0.12.4 (also broken on master since #37907 / dcbe5bdd)\n",
